{
  "description": "Argument-scoped approval — PROTOCOL_SPEC §6.1.6 / §6.1.7 / §6.1.8 / §6.8.1 (spec v1.28.0, apcore#108). Authorization and approval requirement are two orthogonal results: `effect` carries the first, the orthogonal optional `approval` field the second, and the built-in structure-only `arguments` condition decides whether a rule matches THIS call.\n\nDriver contract. Build an ACL from `rules` in order with the given `default_effect`. Supply a governance projection derived from `arguments` (key set, optionally types — never values, §6.1.8 rule 2) by whatever route the SDK uses; §6.1.8 rule 4 leaves that idiomatic. `arguments: null` means supply NO PROJECTION AT ALL, which is a different case from an empty one. Call the STRUCTURED accessor with caller_id/target_id and assert `access`, the approval requirement, and `expected_matched_rule_index` (null when the decision came from `default_effect`). Separately assert the LEGACY boolean returns `expected_legacy_check`: §6.8.1 makes it fail closed on an approval requirement, so allow+required reads as false. Assert the AuditEntry's `handler_error` is non-null exactly where `expected_audit_handler_error_present` says so — §6.3.1 makes it the if-and-only-if marker of an unevaluable condition, which is what keeps 'the handler said no' and 'no answer was obtainable' tellable apart after the fact. Where `expected_validation_finding_path` is non-null, assert `validate_rules()` reports a finding for rule 0 at that condition path — §6.1.8 descends to `arguments.<predicate>` where one predicate is at fault and stops at `arguments` where none can be named.\n\nTwo cases use `default_effect: allow` against the house rule, and say so in their notes: a `deny` rule TAKING EFFECT under §6.1.1 is not observable against a `deny` default, because not-matching produces the same answer.\n\nLesson recorded from the #107 fixture: it reached for `approval` as its example of a rule key 'from a proposal that has not landed', and v1.28.0 landed that proposal, so the case asserted the opposite of the spec. Do not draw negative examples from live proposals.",
  "test_cases": [
    {
      "id": "force_flag_present_requires_approval",
      "note": "THE DRIVING CASE, in its deployment shape: a narrow approval rule ahead of a plain allow. `git push --force` is authorized and must still be put to a human. Before v1.28.0 the only way to express this was to mark the whole module requires_approval and stand down inside the handler, which floods the audit trail and dilutes the annotation.",
      "default_effect": "deny",
      "rules": [
        {
          "callers": [
            "*"
          ],
          "targets": [
            "cli.git_push"
          ],
          "effect": "allow",
          "approval": "required",
          "conditions": {
            "arguments": {
              "has_key": [
                "force"
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "callers": [
            "*"
          ],
          "targets": [
            "cli.git_push"
          ],
          "effect": "allow"
        }
      ],
      "caller_id": "agent.planner",
      "target_id": "cli.git_push",
      "arguments": {
        "remote": "origin",
        "force": true
      },
      "expected_access": "allow",
      "expected_approval_required": true,
      "expected_legacy_check": false,
      "expected_matched_rule_index": 0,
      "expected_audit_handler_error_present": false,
      "expected_validation_finding_path": null
    },
